\usepackage[margin=25mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,booktabs,hyperref}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}
\title{A small incidence classification for unique pentagons}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
An $\operatorname{egr}(n,4,5,1)$ graph is a finite connected simple graph
on $n$ vertices, of degree four and girth five, in which every edge lies
in exactly one pentagon. We recover the following result of Goedgebeur
and Jooken~\cite{source}, stated on page~12 and in Table~2 of the fixed
arXiv v2 edition.
\begin{theorem}
The least order of an $\operatorname{egr}(n,4,5,1)$ graph is $30$.
There is exactly one such graph on $30$ vertices, up to isomorphism.
\end{theorem}
The proof uses the cycle-incidence theorem established in our preceding
note~\cite{incidence}. Its finite part enumerates regular graphs on ten
and twelve vertices. The candidates supplied by a graph generator are
checked for completeness by a separate count of labelled graphs. Thus
neither the source's answer nor the generator's assertion of completeness
is a premise of the enumeration below.

\paragraph{The common incidence theorem.}
We acquire the following precise conclusion from~\cite{incidence}.
In a graph of degree four with exactly one girth cycle of length $g$
through each edge, distinct girth cycles meet in at most one vertex.
Each vertex belongs to exactly two girth cycles. Consequently the graph
$H$ whose vertices are these cycles, with one edge for each original
vertex, is simple and $g$-regular, on $2n/g$ vertices. At each vertex of
$H$, its incident edges inherit an unoriented cyclic order of length $g$.
The original graph has vertex set $E(H)$; two vertices are adjacent
precisely when the corresponding incident edges are consecutive in one
of these local cycles. This reconstruction includes every original graph.
Any isomorphism of the reconstructed graphs preserves their girth cycles
and induces an isomorphism of their incidence graphs. We use the theorem
only with $g=5$.

\begin{lemma}
An $\operatorname{egr}(n,4,5,1)$ graph has $n\geq25$ and $5\mid n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The ball of radius two about a vertex has $1+4+12=17$ vertices.
Girth five makes its radial edges a tree, and every additional edge in
the ball joins two vertices at distance two from the root. Such an edge
corresponds to a pentagon through the root. There are exactly two of
these pentagons, since its four incident edges belong to one each and
a pentagon uses two of them. The twelve vertices at distance two have
$36$ incidences left after their radial edges; the two additional edges
consume four of these. Thus $32$ edges leave the ball. At degree four
they require at least eight further vertices, proving $n\geq25$.
Counting edge incidences with pentagons gives $5c=2n$, whence $5\mid n$.
This is the odd-ball counting argument also used in our acquired lower
bound component; it is not claimed as a new bound.
\end{proof}
It therefore suffices to exclude order $25$ and classify order $30$.
Their incidence graphs have orders ten and twelve, respectively.
We include disconnected bases in the finite search; this enlarges the
candidate set and cannot discard a connected solution.

\paragraph{Certifying the complete list of bases.}
Let $F(d_1,\ldots,d_h)$ be the number of simple graphs on a specified
labelled vertex set having the indicated degrees. With $F(\varnothing)=1$
and $F=0$ for impossible degree entries or an odd degree sum,
\[
 F(d_1,\ldots,d_h)=
 \sum_{\substack{S\subseteq\{2,\ldots,h\}\\|S|=d_1}}
 F\bigl(d_2-\mathbf1_{2\in S},\ldots,d_h-\mathbf1_{h\in S}\bigr).
\]
Indeed $S$ is exactly the neighbour set of the first vertex. Deleting
that vertex is a bijection for each fixed $S$. Permuting the remaining
degree entries does not change the count, by relabelling vertices; the
implementation sorts them solely to reuse equal values of $F$.

We certify that the supplied bases are pairwise nonisomorphic and compute
their exact automorphism orders by the following elementary procedure.
Start with one ordered cell containing every vertex. Repeatedly split
each cell according to the vector of neighbour counts in the current
ordered cells, with the resulting vectors in lexicographic order.
If a cell is not a singleton, choose a smallest such cell, breaking ties
by its position. For each of its vertices, put that vertex in a singleton
before the remaining cell and recurse. A discrete partition gives an
ordered adjacency word. Retain the least word among all leaves and the
number of leaves giving it.

Every step commutes with graph isomorphisms: neighbour counts and ordered
cell positions are preserved, and every possible distinguished vertex
is explored. Hence isomorphic graphs have the same least word. Conversely,
two equal words themselves give a vertex isomorphism. For a fixed graph,
its automorphisms act freely and transitively on the leaves giving one
fixed word: compose the bijections from their vertex orders. Freeness
follows because a complete vertex order has trivial stabilizer, and the
equivariance just proved shows that every automorphism carries a leaf
to a leaf. Thus the recorded multiplicity is $|\operatorname{Aut}H|$.
Disconnected graphs are treated component by component, multiplying
automorphism orders and the factorials of the multiplicities of equal
components. This is valid because every automorphism permutes isomorphic
components and restricts to an isomorphism on each.

The orbit of a base $H$ under labelling has
$h!/|\operatorname{Aut}H|$ elements. Distinct canonical words give disjoint
orbits. Equality of their total size with $F(5,\ldots,5)$ consequently
proves completeness, without assuming anything about the candidate
provider. The exact results are
\[
\begin{array}{r|r|r}
h&\text{nonisomorphic bases}&F(5,\ldots,5)
 =\sum_Hh!/|\operatorname{Aut}H|\\\hline
10&60&66\,462\,606\\
12&7\,849&2\,977\,635\,137\,862.
\end{array}
\]

\paragraph{The local enumeration and its pruning rule.}
At each vertex of a base there are $4!/2=12$ unoriented cycles on its
five incident edges. Fix the first edge and enumerate permutations of
the other four, retaining the orientation whose second edge precedes
its last. This lists every choice exactly once. Process the base vertices
in a fixed order, adding the five edges of each chosen local pentagon.
Different base vertices cannot add the same reconstructed edge: two
distinct edges of a simple base have at most one common endpoint.

Reject a partial graph if a newly added edge lies in a triangle, in a
quadrangle, or in more than one pentagon. Each newly added edge already
lies in its designated pentagon. Short cycles and additional pentagons
persist after further edges are added, so this rejection cannot remove
a valid completion. Conversely, any forbidden cycle first appears at
some addition and contains at least one edge of that addition. Checking
all five new edges therefore detects it. Inductively, a returned complete
assignment has girth five and each edge in exactly one pentagon.

For clarity, the implemented predicate can be written solely in terms of
neighbour sets. For a new edge $uv$, a triangle is detected by
$N(u)\cap N(v)\ne\varnothing$. A quadrangle is detected by an edge
between $N(u)\setminus\{v\}$ and $N(v)\setminus\{u\}$. If neither occurs,
the number of pentagons through $uv$ is
\[
 \sum_{\substack{a\in N(u)\setminus\{v\}\\
                  c\in N(v)\setminus\{u\}}}
 \bigl|(N(a)\cap N(c))\setminus\{u,v\}\bigr|.
\]
The summands count exactly the paths $u,a,z,c,v$. Their vertices are
distinct: $a=c$ would give a triangle through $uv$, the endpoints have
been removed, and loops do not occur. The orientation of $uv$ fixes a
unique such path for every unoriented pentagon. The predicate requires
this integer to be one.

\paragraph{Completion and identification.}
Every base has a recorded opening, its complete depth counts, its
accepted assignments and a closing record. The complete enumeration
returns no assignment on the sixty bases of order ten. On the $7\,849$
bases of order twelve it returns twelve assignments. Every returned
graph is checked afresh for connectivity, degree, girth, and the number
of pentagons through each edge, by direct simple-path enumeration.
All twelve have a retained literal vertex bijection to the graph
\texttt{UntilOrder250\_180} in the source authors' supplementary data.
The verifier checks every adjacency under each bijection. Thus there is
at most one isomorphism class on thirty vertices and at least one.
Together with the exclusion at twenty-five and the lower-bound lemma,
this proves the theorem.

\paragraph{Validation and attribution.}
The finite programs use exact integers. The canonical classification and
automorphism multiplicities are compared with all permutations for every
labelled graph of order at most five: $1\,100$ graphs in $53$ isomorphism
classes. The degree recurrence is checked against those graphs for all
$50$ degree multisets. Independently enumerating all simple paths through
each edge checks the pruning predicate on all $33\,868$ labelled graphs
of order at most six, for $251\,085$ edge occurrences. These controls
test the implementation; the arguments above establish the general
coverage and pruning assertions. A fresh compiled enumeration and a
separate run with memory and undefined-behaviour checks are retained.

The extremal theorem and its positive graph belong to
Goedgebeur and Jooken. The cycle-incidence argument is acquired from our
preceding component; this application supplies the smaller finite
classification and its explicit coverage certificate. No priority claim
for the incidence viewpoint is made. The graph generator and the program
used to find vertex bijections serve only as candidate providers: all
coverage and all returned maps are checked separately. This component
does not settle a new open conjecture, recover the other principal
tables of the source, supply a compression ratio, or constitute a Lean
formalization.

